From 94a2c1fd3a1d6c70fc2bff9517ff2df27818f1f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iceccarelli <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 19:00:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: 32 service areas with an honest core/travel split
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Takes the matrix from 84 to 224 pages, and the site from 181 to 341.

20 new municipalities across Durham, York, Peel, Halton, Hamilton, Simcoe,
Dufferin, and Wellington, each with its own housing-stock and specification
prose — East York's wartime semis with oak under broadloom, Hamilton's Durand
Victorians, Innisfil's cottage conversions that swing humidity all winter.

The structural addition is City.tier:

- core (20)     free on-site measure for any qualified job
- extended (12) we travel, but on stair packages and whole-home work, not a
                single-room repair

That distinction is published on the area page, the matrix page, and the areas
index, and it is priced into cityMult. A test asserts the extended tier averages
above the core multiplier, because a crew losing ninety minutes a day to travel
costs more per square foot. Ranking for 'hardwood stairs Barrie' and then
declining the call is worse than not ranking.

Also: City.titleName for the two municipalities whose full names overflow a
SERP title; homepage and footer now show the core twenty and link to all 32;
the '32 cities' stat is true again.

99 tests. 341 pages, 341 unique titles, 1,919 JSON-LD blocks, clean audit.
